 <h2>What Causes High LDL Cholesterol?</h2>
  <p>High LDL can be influenced by diet, excess weight, physical inactivity, genetics and certain medical conditions or medicines. Familial hypercholesterolemia is an inherited condition that can cause particularly high cholesterol.</p>

  <h2>How Is LDL Cholesterol Tested?</h2>
  <p>LDL cholesterol is commonly included in a lipid profile or lipid panel. The test may also report total cholesterol, HDL cholesterol, triglycerides and other lipid measurements. Whether fasting is required depends on the testing circumstances and your healthcare provider's instructions.</p>

  <h2>How Can You Lower LDL?</h2>
  <p>Lifestyle measures can help improve cholesterol levels. Depending on your individual risk, a healthcare professional may also recommend cholesterol-lowering medicines.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose more vegetables, fruits, whole grains and legumes.</li>
    <li>Reduce foods high in saturated fat.</li>
    <li>Stay physically active.</li>
    <li>Maintain a healthy weight.</li>
    <li>Avoid smoking.</li>
    <li>Follow medical advice about cholesterol-lowering treatment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When Should You Speak With a Doctor?</h2>
  <p>If your LDL remains elevated, is very high, or you have diabetes, cardiovascular disease, a strong family history of high cholesterol or other cardiovascular risk factors, discuss your results with a qualified healthcare professional.</p>

